Q9 — Four-router OSPF enterprise network
Suggested topology: R1—R2—R3—R4, plus a redundant R1—R4 link to show failover.
Each router has a local LAN. Use point-to-point /30 links.
Example LANs:
R1 LAN 192.168.1.0/24; R2 LAN 192.168.2.0/24;
R3 LAN 192.168.3.0/24; R4 LAN 192.168.4.0/24.
Example transit networks:
R1-R2 10.0.12.0/30; R2-R3 10.0.23.0/30;
R3-R4 10.0.34.0/30; R1-R4 10.0.14.0/30.
Assign each interface a unique IP in its stated network, enable `no shutdown`,
and configure serial clock rate on DCE ends.

On EACH router, use OSPF process 1 and advertise the LAN and all directly connected transit networks. Example for R1 (adjust interface IPs and networks to match your build):
conf t
router ospf 1
 router-id 1.1.1.1
 network 192.168.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0
 network 10.0.12.0 0.0.0.3 area 0
 network 10.0.14.0 0.0.0.3 area 0
end

R2:
router ospf 1
 router-id 2.2.2.2
 network 192.168.2.0 0.0.0.255 area 0
 network 10.0.12.0 0.0.0.3 area 0
 network 10.0.23.0 0.0.0.3 area 0

R3:
router ospf 1
 router-id 3.3.3.3
 network 192.168.3.0 0.0.0.255 area 0
 network 10.0.23.0 0.0.0.3 area 0
 network 10.0.34.0 0.0.0.3 area 0

R4:
router ospf 1
 router-id 4.4.4.4
 network 192.168.4.0 0.0.0.255 area 0
 network 10.0.34.0 0.0.0.3 area 0
 network 10.0.14.0 0.0.0.3 area 0

Verification:
show ip ospf neighbor
show ip route ospf
show ip protocols
ping between PCs on different LANs
tracert <remote-PC-IP>

Failover experiment:
1. Run a continuous ping between PCs behind R1 and R3/R4.
2. Disable one link interface with `interface ...` then `shutdown`.
3. Observe OSPF route recalculation and recovery of ping after convergence.
4. Restore the link with `no shutdown`.
Record convergence time using simulation timestamps/ping replies; exact time depends on Packet Tracer settings.
